# Target C-II — fixed-slack near-diameter matchings have bounded noncrossing degree

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the fixed-slack noncrossing-capacity theorem; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** planar unit-separated diameter-`D` sources; application to the SIGNED upper Horn branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** fixed-slack near-diameter close-connector theorem; inactive near-diameter degree bound; upper-blocker dense-grid localization  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_signed_bounded_noncrossing_degree_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The preceding fixed-slack theorem proved that a vertex-disjoint matching of pairs of length at least

\[
D-{A\over D}
\]

has only `O_A(1)` pairwise-noncrossing members. Its proof gives a much stronger pointwise statement which the Ramsey extraction did not use.

Fix one matching edge `AB`. Every other matching edge which does **not** cross `AB` properly must contribute one of its two endpoints to the fixed union

\[
B(A,\sqrt{2A})\cup B(B,\sqrt{2A}).
\]

Because the whole family is a matching, those contributed endpoints are distinct. Unit separation therefore bounds the number of noncrossers of **each fixed edge** by one source-independent constant.

Consequently the complement of the proper-crossing graph has bounded maximum degree. It can be greedily colored with a fixed number of colors, and every color class is a pairwise-properly-crossing braid.

Thus there is no Ramsey exponent loss:

> **Bounded-noncrossing-degree theorem.** For every fixed `A>0`, every vertex-disjoint matching `E` of source pairs satisfying
> \[
> |e|\ge D-{A\over D}
> \]
> can be partitioned into at most
> \[
> \alpha_A
> :=1+\left\lceil 8\left(\sqrt{2A}+{1\over2}\right)^2\right\rceil
> \]
> pairwise-properly-crossing submatchings, for all sufficiently large `D`.
> In particular one crossing submatching has size at least
> \[
> {|E|\over\alpha_A}.
> \]

For the current SIGNED branch, `A=K+1` with `K=31.4e6`, so

\[
\alpha_{K+1}=502{,}463{,}417.
\]

This is a large constant but it is fixed. After the existing `D^{3/4}`-degree matching extraction, a localized SIGNED winner therefore contains a pairwise-crossing braid of size

\[
\boxed{
\left(c_{\rm cross}-o(1)\right){D^{1/4}\over\log D}
=D^{1/4-o(1)},
}
\]

where the conservative explicit coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
c_{\rm cross}
={c_*\over16\,3^{1/8}\,\alpha_{K+1}}
=6.08144862586\ldots\times10^{-20}.}
\]

This replaces the previous `D^{epsilon_K-o(1)}` extraction, where `epsilon_K` was about `4.98e-10`. The earlier statement remains true, but the exponent loss was an artifact of using only bounded independence number instead of the pointwise bounded noncrossing degree already contained in the same geometry.

All tags survive: the stronger braid remains SIGNED, retains injective Horn ownership, and stays in the same COMMON-CUT or LOCAL-WINDOW low-defect train scaffold.

---

## 1. Pointwise noncrossing degree

Fix `A>0`. Let `E` be a vertex-disjoint matching of source pairs in a unit-separated planar source of diameter at most `D`, with

\[
|e|\ge\ell_D:=D-{A\over D}
\qquad(e\in E).
\tag{BD.1}
\]

The previous close-connector theorem states that, for all sufficiently large `D`, if two distinct matching edges

\[
e=AB,
\qquad f=CD
\]

do not cross properly, then

\[
\boxed{
\min\{|A-C|,|A-D|,|B-C|,|B-D|\}<\sqrt{2A}.
}
\tag{BD.2}
\]

Fix `e=AB`. Let `N_0(e)` be the set of other edges in `E` which do not cross `e` properly. By (BD.2), for every `f in N_0(e)` choose one endpoint of `f` lying in

\[
U_e:=B(A,\sqrt{2A})\cup B(B,\sqrt{2A}).
\tag{BD.3}
\]

The chosen source endpoints are distinct because `E` is a matching.

A disk of radius `R` contains at most

\[
4(R+1/2)^2
\tag{BD.4}
\]

points of a unit-separated source: put disjoint radius-`1/2` disks around the source points and compare areas inside the concentric radius-`R+1/2` disk.

With `R=sqrt(2A)`, the two disks in (BD.3) therefore contain at most

\[
Q_A
:=\left\lceil
8\left(\sqrt{2A}+{1\over2}\right)^2
\right\rceil
\tag{BD.5}
\]

source points altogether. Hence

\[
\boxed{|N_0(e)|\le Q_A}
\tag{BD.6}
\]

for **every** matching edge `e`.

This is stronger than bounding the size of a wholly noncrossing submatching.

---

## 2. The complement crossing graph has bounded maximum degree

Make the proper-crossing graph `X(E)` whose vertices are the matching edges and where two vertices are adjacent exactly when the corresponding straight segments cross properly.

Equation (BD.6) says

\[
\boxed{
\Delta(\overline{X(E)})\le Q_A.
}
\tag{BD.7}
\]

Every graph of maximum degree at most `Q_A` is greedily colorable with at most `Q_A+1` colors. Apply this to the complement graph.

A color class is independent in `overline{X(E)}`, hence a clique in `X(E)`. Therefore `E` is the disjoint union of at most

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_A:=Q_A+1
=1+\left\lceil
8\left(\sqrt{2A}+{1\over2}\right)^2
\right\rceil
}
\tag{BD.8}
\]

pairwise-crossing submatchings.

In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\omega(X(E))\ge {|E|\over\alpha_A}.
}
\tag{BD.9}
\]

No Ramsey theorem is needed.

---

## 3. Apply to a localized SIGNED winner

The upper-blocker localization theorem gives, whenever SIGNED wins, a family of more than

\[
(c_*-o(1)){D\over\log D}
\tag{BD.10}
\]

distinct SIGNED upper blockers inside one COMMON-CUT or LOCAL-WINDOW low-defect scaffold, where

\[
c_*={791\over1{,}410{,}278{,}400{,}000}
=5.60882163408\ldots\times10^{-10}.
\tag{BD.11}
\]

Every blocker obeys

\[
D-{K+o(1)\over D}<|e|<D,
\qquad
K=31.4\times10^6.
\tag{BD.12}
\]

The generic inactive near-diameter graph has

\[
\Delta\le(8\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}.
\tag{BD.13}
\]

Greedy matching therefore extracts a vertex-disjoint matching `M_D` with

\[
\boxed{
|M_D|
\ge
\left({c_*\over16\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)
{D^{1/4}\over\log D}.
}
\tag{BD.14}
\]

Take

\[
A=K+1.
\]

For all sufficiently large `D`, every edge of `M_D` satisfies (BD.1). Equation (BD.9) gives a pairwise-properly-crossing submatching `F_D` with

\[
\boxed{
|F_D|
\ge
\left(
{c_*\over16\,3^{1/8}\,\alpha_{K+1}}
-o(1)
\right){D^{1/4}\over\log D}.
}
\tag{BD.15}

The fixed constant evaluates to

\[
\alpha_{K+1}=502{,}463{,}417,
\tag{BD.16}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
{c_*\over16\,3^{1/8}\,\alpha_{K+1}}
=6.08144862586\ldots\times10^{-20}.
}
\tag{BD.17}

The coefficient is irrelevant asymptotically; the exponent is now `1/4-o(1)`.

---

## 4. Metadata and downstream effect

Every step from the localized blocker family to `F_D` takes subsets only. Therefore the stronger braid retains:

- the fixed SIGNED projection
  \[
  r_e\cdot w_*
  \le-\left({1\over4K}-o(1)\right)D;
  \]
- the original distinct Horn owner coordinates;
- the exact path-support interval of every blocker;
- whichever COMMON-CUT or LOCAL-WINDOW train scaffold supplied the localized family.

Thus every later SIGNED theorem which used only the existence of a `D^{epsilon_K-o(1)}` pairwise-crossing braid may replace that starting population by

\[
\boxed{D^{1/4-o(1)}}.
\tag{BD.18}
\]

In particular, the current COMMON-CUT robust-owner-grid and COLUMN-UNIQUE fence-cell reductions inherit the stronger starting scale without changing their geometric proofs. A separate note may re-optimize their displayed exponents and logarithmic cable constants; no such numerical re-optimization is needed for (BD.18).

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The SIGNED branch no longer pays a microscopic Ramsey exponent merely to obtain crossing order. Its localized upper obstruction has the normal form

```text
D/log D SIGNED near-diameter blockers in one low-defect scaffold
-> D^(1/4)/log D vertex-disjoint near-diameter matching
-> after one fixed-factor coloring,
   D^(1/4)/log D pairwise-properly-crossing SIGNED braid.
```

The remaining SIGNED obstruction is therefore genuinely polynomial at exponent `1/4`, on both the COMMON-CUT and LOCAL-WINDOW sides. The next useful work is metric/order closure inside the associated train scaffold, not another crossing-family extraction.

**Disposition:** upgraded fixed-slack noncrossing capacity to bounded pointwise noncrossing degree and removed the Ramsey exponent loss from the SIGNED upper branch. C-II and Level I remain open.
